#title()

We are an independent group of researchers trying to approach machine learning,
natural and artificial intelligence from a different angle.

We have spent more than 10 years of our lives on it, so it better be something
genuinely interesting. And boy it is!

So far we have published #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.00869")[our first
paper] in 2025, with more to come.

We are self funding our research, and now looking for ways to finally make it
profitable. This site is one of the steps toward this goal.

= What we do, actually?

We are researching a so called discrete approach to machine learning that is not
based on artificial neural networks or large language models. It aims to
overcome ANN and LLM limitations by fully embracing the active memory similar to
human's.

Everything, from model vision to factual knowledge is represented by individual
memory elements that can be adjusted or edited at will, without affecting the
rest of the model's memory.

This is quite different from mainstream ANN or LLM, so we were forced to build
all the tools and many of the algorithms from scratch.

Of course, it's only part of the story. Others include tricks for effective
tokenization, dimensionality reduction, topological transformation and semantic
extraction. Together, they pave the way to infinitely extensible agentic memory.
I mean, _true active memory_, and not just another fancy RAG.

Many AI startups confuse a memory with a notebook or a scratchpad. But the true
memory is anything but that. It "knows" by itself when to recall something and
when to forget. Sure, you can scribble your grocery list on a sticker note, but
this won't work for any knowledge. By having a math textbook laying on your table
you can answer many questions simply by looking them up in the book, but that
alone would not make you a better mathematician. What you really need is to gain
knowledge and experience by reading and _internalizing_ the book, and working
through the problems. Later, you could still use the book as a reference, but
this would be is very different, since by that time you would already have
general understanding of the concepts inside the book.

Our goal is to create a memory system that would work similar to human memory
and could store _experience_, and not just random _facts_.

All combined, this would eventually help us achieve our practical goals —
discrete transformer, and sustainable AGI.

In the longer run our ultimate goal is to explain how the real brain works.
Previously our group had contacts with Bekhtereva's Institute of the Human Brain,
so our core ideas were evaluated in terms of biological viability.

= Why?

Among other things, this is especially important for safety and alignment
purposes. Imagine, you need a voice assistant for your business that would answer
phone calls and arrange meetings. Surely, to do that the model must be able to
speak and chat fluently and absolutely should know the manners, be able to talk
with the customers politely, interpret their intents and overall behave
human-like.

At the same, time no one would expect such an assistant to be proficient in
quantum physics, neuroscience or hacking, right? Yet, that's exactly what happens
now. Companies spend millions of dollars training super intelligence on all human
knowledge only to let other companies put it behind a reception desk. Weird.

By using our tech we'd be able to reduce the model's knowledge to the bare
minimum, keeping the important parts and blocking or physically removing
everything else that is not relevant, including copyrighted material, swear
words, #strike[memes,] sensitive or otherwise inappropriate content.
